\section{Excess Kurtosis of Exponential Distribution}
Tags: Exponential Distribution, Kurtosis

\begin{theorem}
Let $X$ be a continuous random variable of the exponential distribution with parameter $\beta$ for some $\beta \in \R_{> 0}$.
Then the excess kurtosis $\gamma_2$ of $X$ is equal to $6$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From the definition of excess kurtosis, we have: 
:$\gamma_2 = \expect {\paren {\dfrac {X - \mu} \sigma}^4} - 3$
where: 
:$\mu$ is the expectation of $X$. 
:$\sigma$ is the standard deviation of $X$.
By Expectation of Exponential Distribution we have: 
:$\mu = \beta$ 
By Variance of Exponential Distribution we have: 
:$\sigma = \beta$
So: 
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn	| l = \gamma_2
	| r = \dfrac {\expect {X^4} - 4 \mu \expect {X^3} + 6 \mu^2 \expect {X^2} - 3 \mu^4} {\sigma^4} - 3
	| c = Kurtosis in terms of Non-Central Moments
}}
{{eqn	| r = \frac {4! \beta^4 - 4 \beta \paren {3! \beta^3} + 6 \beta^2 \paren {2! \beta^2} - 3 \beta^4} {\beta^4} - 3
	| c = Raw Moment of Exponential Distribution
}}
{{eqn	| r = \frac {24 \beta^4 - 24 \beta^4 + 12 \beta^4 - 3 \beta^4} {\beta^4} - 3
}}
{{eqn	| r = \frac {9 \beta^4} {\beta^4} - 3
}}
{{eqn	| r = 6
}}
{{end-eqn}}
{{qed}}
Category:Kurtosis
Category:Exponential Distribution
\end{proof}

